From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 16:37:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/amd/display: the static HDR packet of a replaced stream
 on a pipe that DC keeps

DC programs again only the pipes that pipe_need_reprogram() names, and it
compares no InfoFrame. Where a commit replaces the stream of a CRTC with the
same timing, colour space, signal and DPMS state, the stream encoder keeps the
packets of the old stream. linux-9910 (the Dolby Vision tunnel) and linux-9918
(the vendor InfoFrame of HDMI 3D) send theirs with the stream update of
amdgpu_dm_commit_planes() for that reason. The static HDR packet (the Dynamic
Range and Mastering InfoFrame, or its SDP on DisplayPort) had nothing of the
kind: the display could stay in HDR over an SDR picture, or show HDR as SDR.
CB1 found it on a Ryzen 7430U (CroqueMr/libreelec-CB1, PR #306, 94a0dbadf8,
"linux-9980-amd-refresh-hdmi-static-hdr"), with a direct atomic test.

How often it happens in this tree, by the code: entering or leaving HDR forces
a modeset (amdgpu_dm_connector_atomic_check()), and dm_update_crtc_state()
takes it back where dc_is_stream_unchanged() finds the new stream the same as
the old one; the packet then goes as a stream update of its own, as it always
did. A change of colour space or depth changes the timing as DC compares it
(dc_is_timing_changed()), and the pipe is programmed in full. What is left is
a modeset that stays for what dc_is_stream_unchanged() compares and
pipe_need_reprogram() does not (the audio of the sink, the MSA timing
parameter) or for a change of connectors (scaling, ABM), at the same timing:
rare, and then wrong until the next modeset.

So the packet goes with the update there too: where the commit replaced the
stream, the pipe is one that DC keeps (same signal, DPMS state, timing and
colour space) and the packet of the new stream differs from that of the old
one (amdgpu_dm_hdr_packet_kept_stale()). DC rebuilds every InfoFrame of the
stream for it (resource_build_info_frame()). A pipe that DC programs in full is
left alone, so nothing is sent twice. The kernel log says each time what the
packet was and is ("static HDR packet: none -> PQ (HDR10), sent with the
update ..."). Done the way of 9910 and 9918, not in dce110_apply_ctx_to_hw()
as CB1 does.

The same fault was also found and fixed in amdgpu_dm_commit_planes() for
every replaced stream, with the AVI InfoFrame as well, for an SDR picture
that goes out in BT.2020 after an HDR10 one (Kodi then tells the display
SDR by an HDR InfoFrame whose EOTF is that of SDR, which must reach it).
That is a pipe that DC keeps (the same timing, depth and colour space,
BT.2020) whose packet differs, so it is covered here, and this is the one
implementation of both. The AVI InfoFrame needs nothing of its own: a change
of the colour space is a change of the timing for DC (dc_is_timing_changed()
compares output_color_space), so the pipe and every InfoFrame of it are
programmed anew, and the AVI packet that a stream carries (avi_infopacket)
is only that of the Dolby Vision tunnel, which linux-9910 sends.

Ported to Linux 7.4: the KUnit cases go after the tests that 7.4 adds.

KUnit (amdgpu_dm_connector_test): SDR to HDR10 and back, HDR10 to HLG, the same
packet, no old stream, and another depth, colour space, signal or DPMS state,
which DC programs in full; the EOTF named from the InfoFrame and from the SDP.

Not tried on hardware.
